@node	0.7, 0.8, 0.6, Chapter 0
@section Testing

This section covers the test framework, property tests, how solutions are
checked, and the pending marker. The rig is small on purpose: vitest for
structure and assertions, fast-check where a property beats an example,
and one recipe that reports what is still unsolved.

@heading Anatomy of a test

A test file sits beside the code it proves, in the same directory, with
the name the listing carries plus @file{.test.ts}. It imports
@code{describe}, @code{it}, and @code{expect} from vitest. Table-style
tests register one test per row with @code{it.each}:

@example typescript
const parity = (n: number): string => (n % 2 === 0 ? "even" : "odd");

it.each([
  [0, "even"],
  [1, "odd"],
  [42, "even"],
])("parity(%i) is %s", (n, expected) => @{
  expect(parity(n)).toBe(expected);
@});
@end example

@noindent
A property test states what must hold for every generated input and lets
the framework try to break it. fast-check draws values, shrinks a failing
sample to a minimal case, and reports the first counterexample. Boundary
cases that generation would rarely hit stay explicit:

@example typescript
it("a property test runs the assertion over generated inputs", () => @{
  fc.assert(
    fc.property(
      fc.integer(@{ min: -100, max: 100 @}),
      fc.integer(@{ min: -100, max: 100 @}),
      (a, b) => @{
        expect(absDiff(a, b)).toBe(absDiff(b, a));
      @},
    ),
  );
@});

it("boundary cases stay explicit", () => @{
  expect(absDiff(0, 0)).toBe(0);
  expect(
    absDiff(Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER, Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER),
  ).toBe(0);
@});
@end example

@noindent
The two listings live in @file{packages/ch0/src/07-testing.ts} and
@file{07-testing.test.ts}. Where the book needs randomness, it does not
reach for the host generator: the edition ships a seeded xorshift source,
so the stochastic exercises of @ref{1.2} and @ref{3.1} give the same
transcript on every machine and every run. Chapters 1 and 2 test plain
functions with plain @code{it} and @code{expect}; from @ref{3.1} the
tests use @code{@@effect/vitest}, the Effect-aware runner, because the
code under test works in @code{Effect} values.

@heading How solutions are checked

Exercises and solutions share file names, and a test run sees both: the
scaffold's @code{test.todo} is reported and skipped, the solution's test
runs for real. @command{just test} runs every chapter's suite, and the
gate stays green while work is pending, because a todo is a report, not
a failure:

@example
just scaffold   # pnpm vitest run --reporter=verbose, todo lines only
@end example

@noindent
@command{just scaffold} prints the pending list; it reports, it does not
gate. The full gate is @command{just lint}: @command{biome check} over
every file, formatting and lint in one pass, and @code{tsc --noEmit} in
every chapter package, where the strict settings of @ref{0.1} bite. A @code{test.todo} keeps the suite's exit code green; a failing
assertion does not.

@cindex vitest
@cindex fast-check
@cindex test.todo
